course: space
lesson: 6
title: The Moon
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The Moon is a ball of grey rock about a quarter as wide as the Earth, circling us about 384,000 kilometres away, the width of about 30 Earths in a row.
explain: It spins exactly once each time it goes round us, like a dancer who keeps facing her partner, so we always see the same face.
explain: The dark patches people call seas are plains of old hardened lava, and the rest is scarred with craters left by rocks from space.
explain: There is almost no air, so on the Moon the sky is black even by day, there is no wind and no sound, and footprints can last for millions of years.
explain: Gravity there is only one sixth of ours: a 30-kilogram child would feel as light as 5 kilograms and could bound like a kangaroo.
fact: The Moon is about a quarter of the Earth's width and orbits about 384,000 kilometres away, a distance of about 30 Earths lined up.
fact: The Moon always shows the same face to the Earth because it spins once in exactly the time it takes to orbit, about 27 days.
fact: The far side of the Moon is not dark; it gets just as much sunlight as the near side, we simply never see it from the Earth.
fact: The dark patches on the Moon are called maria, Latin for seas, but they are plains of ancient hardened lava, not water.
fact: The Moon is covered in craters, some hundreds of kilometres wide, made by asteroids and comets hitting it.
fact: Gravity on the Moon is about one sixth of the Earth's, so a 30-kilogram child would feel as light as 5 kilograms.
fact: The Moon has almost no atmosphere, so there is no sound, no wind and the sky is black even in daytime.
fact: Twelve astronauts walked on the Moon in six Apollo landings between 1969 and 1972, starting with Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin.
fact: In April 2026 the Artemis II mission was reported to have flown four astronauts around the Moon, the first crew to go there since 1972; they did not land.
fact: Water ice exists in permanently shadowed craters near the Moon's poles.
fact: The Moon is slowly drifting away from the Earth by about 3.8 centimetres a year.
fact: The Moon makes no light of its own; it shines by reflecting sunlight.
exercise: How wide is the Moon compared with the Earth? | About a quarter of the Earth's width | About half the Earth's width | About the same width | About a hundredth of the Earth's width
exercise: How far away is the Moon? | About 384,000 kilometres | About 150 million kilometres | About 3,840 kilometres | About 38 kilometres
exercise: About how many Earths would fit in a line between the Earth and the Moon? | About 30 | About 3 | About 300 | About 3,000
exercise: Why do we always see the same face of the Moon? | It spins once in exactly the time it takes to orbit | It does not spin at all | The Earth's light hides the other side | The other side is always in darkness
exercise: About how long does the Moon take to spin once? | About 27 days | About 24 hours | About one year | It never spins
exercise: Is the far side of the Moon always dark? | No, it gets just as much sunlight as the near side | Yes, the Sun never reaches it | Yes, it is covered in black rock that soaks up all the light | No, it is lit by the Earth only
exercise: What are the dark patches on the Moon? | Plains of ancient hardened lava called maria | Seas of salty water that sparkle in the sunlight | Huge forests and grasslands seen from very far away | Shadows thrown by the tall mountains all around them
exercise: What made the Moon's craters? | Asteroids and comets hitting it | Volcanoes blowing their tops off | Astronauts digging for rock | Rain wearing holes in the ground
exercise: How strong is gravity on the Moon? | About one sixth of the Earth's | About the same as the Earth's, or near it | About twice as strong as the Earth's | There is no gravity up there at all
exercise: A 30-kilogram child on the Moon would feel as light as about... | 5 kilograms | 15 kilograms | 30 kilograms | 0 kilograms
exercise: Why is there no sound on the Moon? | It has almost no air to carry sound | The grey dusty rocks soak up all the sound | It is far too cold there for sound to travel | Sound is blocked out by the glare of the Sun
exercise: What colour is the Moon's sky in daytime? | Black | Blue | Grey | Orange
exercise: How many astronauts have walked on the Moon? | Twelve | Two | One hundred | None
exercise: Who were the first two people to walk on the Moon? | Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin | Yuri Gagarin and Valentina Tereshkova | Isaac Newton and Galileo | Tim Peake and Chris Hadfield
exercise: What did Artemis II do in April 2026? | Flew four astronauts around the Moon without landing | Landed the first woman on the Moon | Built a base on the Moon | Sent a robot to dig at the poles
exercise: Where is there water ice on the Moon? | In shadowed craters near its poles | Deep down in the dark maria, the old lava seas | All over its far side, which is always dark | Nowhere at all, because it is far too hot
exercise: What is happening to the Moon's distance from the Earth? | It drifts away by about 3.8 centimetres a year | It comes closer by a metre a year | It stays exactly the same forever | It moves away by about a kilometre every single year
test: my sister says the dark side of the Moon is always pitch black. right? | No, the far side gets sunlight too; we just never see it from here | Yes, the Sun never shines on it | Yes, that is why it is called the dark side | No, it glows with its own light
test: In a cartoon, astronauts on the Moon hear a loud bang when a rock falls. What is wrong? | With almost no air there is no sound | Nothing, sound is louder on the Moon | Rocks cannot fall on the Moon | They would hear it only at night
test: Old maps call a Moon patch the Sea of Tranquillity. Could you go swimming there? | No, it is a plain of old hardened lava | Yes, it is shallow salty water | Yes, but only at full Moon | No, the water is frozen solid all the way down
test: If you weigh your 30-kilogram backpack on the Moon, how heavy would it feel? | Like about 5 kilograms | Like about 180 kilograms | Exactly 30 kilograms | It would float away
test: Why can astronauts' footprints stay on the Moon for millions of years? | There is no wind or rain to wipe them away | The Moon's dust is glued down hard by the cold | The astronauts painted them on to make them last | The Moon's ground is frozen hard like concrete
test: grandad says we never went back after Apollo, nobody's been near the Moon since. still true? | No, Artemis II flew a crew around the Moon in April 2026 | Yes, nobody has gone near it since 1972 | No, people live on the Moon now | Yes, the Moon is off-limits by law
test: Why does a dancer who keeps facing her partner explain the Moon's face? | The Moon turns once each orbit, so the same side always faces us | The Moon dances round the Sun | The Moon stops spinning when it faces us | The Moon moves its face to watch us
test: Why might future Moon bases be built near the poles? | There is water ice in shadowed craters there | It is warmer at the poles | Gravity is much stronger at the poles, so it is safer | The poles are made of gold
test: What would happen to the Moon's distance over a billion years? | It would be noticeably further away, drifting about 3.8 cm each year | It would crash into the Earth | It would stay exactly as it is | It would fly off into space at once
test: Tom says the Moon is a light, like a giant lamp. What is the truth? | It reflects sunlight; it makes no light of its own | He is right, it burns like a giant candle up in the sky | It glows from its hot volcanoes | It is lit by the stars behind it
test: If you lined up Earths from here to the Moon, about how many would you need? | About 30 | About 3 | About 300 | About 1,000
exam: State the reason for which the same hemisphere of the Moon is perpetually presented to the Earth. | Its period of rotation is equal to its period of revolution | It does not rotate about its axis whatsoever | The opposite hemisphere receives no illumination | The Earth's atmosphere obscures the opposite hemisphere
exam: Identify the nature of the dark regions of the lunar surface designated maria. | Plains of ancient solidified lava | Bodies of standing water | Regions of dense vegetation | Shadows cast by the lunar mountains
exam: Ascertain the approximate gravitational acceleration at the lunar surface relative to that of the Earth. | Approximately one sixth | Approximately one half | Approximately equal | Approximately twice as great
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the crewed exploration of the Moon? | Twelve persons walked upon it in six landings between 1969 and 1972 | No person has at any time set foot upon it | A single person walked upon it in 1969 only | Twelve landings took place during the year 2026
